\section{Join Congruences Without Dropping Common Information}

The arithmetic of a congruence join needs a convention for
singletons. Represent one nonempty congruence by $(r,m)$ with
$m\ge0$: positive $m$ denotes all integers congruent to $r$
modulo $m$, and $m=0$ denotes just $\{r\}$. Bottom is a separate
value. We want the most precise congruence covering both inputs;
the result need not be their exact union. Use mathematical integers
or enough precision to compute residue differences without overflow.
Bounds, if present in a separate interval component, are joined
under their own rules and may then be reduced.

\begin{sudocode}
\Require Congruence elements $a$ and $b$
\If{$a=\bot$}
  \State \Return $b$
\ElsIf{$b=\bot$}
  \State \Return $a$
\EndIf
\To{A common modulus divides both strides and the offset.}
\Let{d}{\gcd(a.m,b.m,|a.r-b.r|)}
\If{$d=0$}
  \To{Equal singletons have no positive stride to normalize.}
  \State \Return $(a.r,0)$
\EndIf
\State \Return $(a.r\bmod d,d)$
\end{sudocode}

For $(2,6)$ and $(8,9)$ the residue difference is $6$ and
$\gcd(6,9,6)=3$. The result is $(2,3)$: the input members
$2,8,17$ all satisfy it, and it also admits $5$, which was in
neither input. A regression joining the singletons $5$ and
$11$ should return $(5,6)$; joining $5$ with itself should
instead return $(5,0)$. Taking only the greatest common divisor
of the strides would fail for those distinct singletons.

For a positive modulus to cover both inputs it must divide every
difference of values within either input and the difference between
the representatives. The computed modulus is a multiple of every
such divisor and itself satisfies all three restrictions, so its class
is contained in every congruence cover. A modulus of one is the
unrestricted integer domain, not bottom. For small inputs, enumerate
bounded samples from each input and assert containment. Samples
can expose a wrong stride, but minimality follows from the divisor
argument, not from passing the enumeration.

\section{A Loop Invariant and a Convergence Test}

Consider $x:=0$ followed by a loop that adds $2$ while $x<9$, with mathematical integer semantics. At the header use the candidate $([0,10],0\bmod 2)$. On the continuing edge the guard gives an upper bound of $8$, and adding $2$ gives $([2,10],0\bmod 2)$. Joining with entry preserves the candidate. On the exit edge, the interval alone is $[9,10]$; the reduction removes $9$, showing that the loop returns exactly $10$. Here the transfer proof uses parity, but the bound keeps parity useful: without the bound there would be no unique exit value.

Figure~\ref{fig:visual-211} shows how the interval and congruence components together identify the only possible exit value.

