For an analyzer regression test, require that the reported header and exit annotations pass the edge checks, then check whether the exit precision is a singleton. A plain interval widening might extrapolate the first increasing upper bound to infinity; choosing $10$ as a widening threshold in this example allows a stable finite candidate. More generally, applying a reduction after every widening may undo the progress that ensured termination, so the product iteration needs its own convergence argument or a schedule that restricts reduction during widening. Once a post-fixpoint has been obtained, later tightening must still end with a sound post-fixpoint. Exhaustively testing small intervals and moduli against direct enumeration checks the exchange code independently of the fixpoint machinery. If the actual IR has wrapping arithmetic, the mathematical transfer and the exchange between domains must also respect wraparound; for example, residues modulo $3$ are not preserved by adding $3$ when the result wraps modulo $256$.
